00:00:12.478 --> 00:00:13.830
- Alright, thank you everybody for coming.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.170
If we have anybody who's
still in the memorabilia room,

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.003
the exhibition hall,

00:00:18.003 --> 00:00:20.490
we encourage you to come
into the lecture hall here

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:25.110
and join us as we kick off
the third annual symposium

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:29.960
on hybrid scholarship at Yale
University: Beyond Boundaries.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.900
And this event is one that
is organized every year

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.760
by a team led by Kathy DeRose

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.460
who's the manager of the
Digital Humanities Lab.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.370
This event for the last three years

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:45.310
really has been the work of Kathy

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.020
and it's been supported
by a great team of others

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.200
inside and outside the lab.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.720
We're especially happy to
work alongside Yale STEAM

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.401
which is a student led group
here at Yale University.

00:00:56.401 --> 00:00:58.450
STEAM, as most of you know,

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.010
stands for STEM plus arts and humanities

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.270
with a silent H.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.200
We're really excited to
be working with them.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.190
It helped us in putting
together the program

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.350
for today's events.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.340
So the exciting thing about
Beyond Boundaries of course

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:13.610
is that it brings together

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.410
folks from all parts of
the scholarly spectrum

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:17.470
at Yale University

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.810
and today you're gonna hear talks

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.610
in round table discussions,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:24.500
for folks who work in our robust museums,

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.290
galleries and libraries organizations,

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.487
you're gonna hear from undergraduates,

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:29.580
graduate students and faculty.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.900
It's really exciting
to have all these parts

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.970
of what makes Yale
University great here today.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.810
When Kathy asked me to
welcome you all to the hall,

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.570
I asked her what are
the things I need to do

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.320
because you're organized everything,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.030
you've managed to get the room renovated,

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.270
all this great program, really excited.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.620
What could I possibly
do that would be useful?

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.077
And she said, "Two things.

00:01:49.077 --> 00:01:50.057
"The first thing to do

00:01:50.057 --> 00:01:52.160
"is to make sure that
everybody's powerpoint works."

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.642
So I'm going to be up here

00:01:53.642 --> 00:01:56.530
as we switch between different
keynote presentations,

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:57.430
different lightning talks

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:00.090
to make sure that everybody's
powerpoint is working well.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.650
And she said the other
thing you need to do

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:02.940
is to keep us on track.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.570
So for all of our wonderful
lightning talk presenters,

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.790
I'm gonna have little one minute warnings

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:11.220
and then this is the cane
that yanks you off the stage

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.000
just so we can get
through everybody's work.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.970
And so with that, I'd like to
kick off our lightning talks

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:18.940
which are gonna be
presentations by students

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.860
and post doctoral associates

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.160
on subjects related to digital humanities

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.880
and STEAM projects here at Yale.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.930
And the first of our lightning talks

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:31.420
is going to be science stories using IIIF

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:32.480
and Wikidata

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.050
to create a linked data web application.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:36.470
And this is gonna be a presentation

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.850
by Kenneth Seals-Nutt and Kat Thornton.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.933
So Ken and Kat, come on up.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.012
- Hello everyone.

00:02:46.012 --> 00:02:48.350
Thank you for allowing me to
speak at this opportunity.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.370
My name is Kenneth Seals-Nutt.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.630
I'm a senior, a computer science major

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.070
and I'm working in
concurrent with Kat Thornton

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:59.230
who is a CLIR fellow of computer science

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:01.400
as well as in the digital
preservation department

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.905
in the library.

00:03:02.905 --> 00:03:06.780
And what we have been
working on this past year

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.940
is called science stories

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.030
and what that is is a application

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.970
that we're gonna leverage two resources

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:17.560
that are widely used by
other projects here at Yale.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:18.393
The first being Wikidata

00:03:18.393 --> 00:03:21.330
and the second being the IIIF framework

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:24.960
and I'll touch a little
bit on what each one is

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.920
as well as give a brief demo

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.355
of the application and
what we've built so far.

00:03:29.355 --> 00:03:31.530
So first off I'd like to start

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.170
by just explaining the team again.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:37.890
Kat Thornton is a CLIR
fellow of computer science,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.810
I'm a senior undergrad student here

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.770
and this also doubles as my senior project

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:46.140
for the computer science department

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.760
and Professor Benedict Brown

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.173
is our faculty advisor for this.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:55.790
So I'll first give the high
level goals of the project

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:57.322
and then break it down

00:03:57.322 --> 00:04:01.220
into what things are being
used within the project.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.510
So our first goal

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.600
is making a visual
storytelling application

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:07.510
of academic research.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:09.051
One thing that we found

00:04:09.051 --> 00:04:14.051
with today's great resources of computing

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.010
as well as a lot of frameworks

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.238
that have been built on the web

00:04:18.238 --> 00:04:19.830
is that we can renovate

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:21.610
how we go about explaining

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.650
our scientific and academic
research to the public.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:26.620
One thing that we found

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:30.040
is we all do great research
here in these institutions

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.630
but if someone that doesn't
have access to Yale's catalog

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.060
or another catalog of interests,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.640
it's really difficult and cumbersome

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.360
to find out this information.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.520
So our first goal was using
images, videos, multimedia

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.470
and incorporating that
with our academic research

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:49.960
to paint the picture of who
we're actually researching

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.500
and provide more contextual
information there.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.330
And then we're also gonna expand this out

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:00.089
using the Wikimedia to
crowdsource some of the content.

00:05:00.089 --> 00:05:05.089
One thing that is really beneficial

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.350
of a lot of research projects

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.150
is these stories can be told

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.710
and a lot of people don't have
to do all of the busy work

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:16.010
or all of the overhead work

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:17.580
of finding out all of the information

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:19.350
that other people have already researched.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:23.080
So if we can find a way to
leverage more crowdsource content

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.920
and incorporate that with scholarly

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.170
and academic research,

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.990
we'll be able to tell
more about the person

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:35.563
and I'll also demo how IIIF connects

00:05:35.563 --> 00:05:38.530
a lot of research institutions
together through this

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:40.730
and allows for authorship

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:42.480
as well as annotations and citations

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.289
to be transferred from
institution to institution

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:48.360
in a very safe way

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.910
that allows a lot of interactivity
for the users as well.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.540
And so ultimately the goal

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.390
is to bridge the gap between information

00:05:58.390 --> 00:05:59.630
that's currently in print,

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:02.760
digitizing that and putting it on the web

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.300
and making it accessible

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.750
and the project as a whole,

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:10.065
the main reason why we
decided to go about this

00:06:10.065 --> 00:06:13.240
is to celebrate 150 years of women at Yale

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:14.690
which is coming up next year.

00:06:17.709 --> 00:06:20.080
So what we wanted to do

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:25.080
is Kat has been doing research
on the early women at Yale

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:27.350
for the past two years

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:28.990
and she approached me

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.280
with the research that she's been doing

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.850
and I thought it was
completely fascinating

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.588
and we both came to the consensus

00:06:34.588 --> 00:06:35.670
that more people

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:37.443
should be able to know
about this information

00:06:37.443 --> 00:06:39.810
and not just be able to
read a paper about it

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:41.948
but also be able to interact with it.

00:06:41.948 --> 00:06:46.100
So we're starting this project
on these first five women

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.050
because they have resources

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:52.260
in a lot of different
areas on Yale's campus

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.450
and so we're starting here

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:58.240
to show that we can leverage
all of Yale's research

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.070
across the library as well
as the Peabody Museum,

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:03.590
as well as the department and the faculty

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:06.470
to all have a part in telling the story,

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.923
not just a library or not
just a museum on campus.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.230
And so first, I'll explain a little bit

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:15.150
about what Wikidata is

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:16.940
for those of you all who do not know.

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:19.560
A lot of people are
familiar with Wikipedia,

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:20.510
the English Wikipedia.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:24.840
Wikidata is another one of the projects

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.850
that the Wikimedia Foundation has adopted

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:32.850
and what it is is it's machine
readable open link data

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.510
and that's a long phrase

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:39.170
but essentially it allows people
to create these statements

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:40.703
and my mouse shows,

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:45.170
for example, this is Grace Hopper's item

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:46.933
on Wikidata right now.

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:48.880
And so it's very clear statements

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.880
of what the bare bones
data is about this person.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.990
And it goes on pretty lengthy

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:58.990
but it just gives you an idea.

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:01.610
So for example, like
instance of human, image

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:03.140
and then it has the link to it,

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:04.900
sex, gender, female.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:08.270
So now we can feed that
information to a computer

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:11.156
and it's all unique identified

00:08:11.156 --> 00:08:13.913
with these identifiers here.

00:08:14.803 --> 00:08:17.710
So that allows not only humans to be able

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.640
to read and understand this information

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.050
but also using algorithms
to curate this data

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.670
as well as to know that you've hit

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.660
exactly what you're looking for

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:27.793
in terms of research.

00:08:28.976 --> 00:08:30.980
And then we're coupling this

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.750
with using IIIF

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:33.583
which stands for

00:08:33.583 --> 00:08:37.100
the International Image
Interoperability Framework

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:37.933
and what that does

00:08:37.933 --> 00:08:41.648
is it's a standard set of image APIs

00:08:41.648 --> 00:08:44.830
that a lot of research
institutions came together

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:48.020
to determine this is how we're gonna

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:50.750
transfer the meta data about our pictures

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.010
when we're sharing these images.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.580
So instead of having
people just copy and paste

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.530
or download images off the web,

00:08:56.530 --> 00:08:59.960
now you can export a JSON package

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:02.200
that has all of the meta data.

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:03.750
So that includes the licensing,

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.060
descriptions of what is
actually in the images

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:10.250
outside of just a caption,

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.393
links to where this resource is.

00:09:13.325 --> 00:09:15.830
For example, if it's digitized,

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:18.740
we can link back to what
the actual collection is

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:21.388
in the archive that it's
being referenced to.

00:09:21.388 --> 00:09:24.870
So I like this picture on the side here

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:27.190
'cause it shows how we
can make this practical.

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:31.300
For example, this is a display
in Boston College right now

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:34.410
where as you know,

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:37.140
you can't really have the public handle

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.530
really old manuscripts and text

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:41.480
so this would give you the possibility

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:42.710
to digitize that information

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:46.300
and have users flip through
the book page by page

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:48.690
in a much more interactive
and personal manner

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:53.690
than just by staring at
whatever page that's there.

00:09:54.941 --> 00:09:57.730
And so I will quickly just explain

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.550
that what our application does

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.500
is it's going to give you the ability

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:07.500
to combine both the information
on the Wikimedia Foundation,

00:10:08.339 --> 00:10:11.561
YouTube videos, as well as books

00:10:11.561 --> 00:10:15.651
and any other content that we
can find all into one place.

00:10:15.651 --> 00:10:20.651
And I'll just do a brief
demo of what that looks like.

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:23.863
Right now, this is in a prototype phase.

00:10:24.879 --> 00:10:26.975
So it takes you to this landing page

00:10:26.975 --> 00:10:29.180
and right off the bat,

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.150
it shows the first five women

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:34.300
that we're planning to do research on

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.940
and when you click on them it shows you

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:38.460
a little description about who there is

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:40.600
and it's pulling this
information from Wikipedia

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.490
and then you can view their story here.

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:45.420
And we decided to go the slides method.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.160
So you click through

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:49.382
and these are all not just for one person,

00:10:49.382 --> 00:10:50.330
these are just to show the extent

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.820
of how the application is gonna work.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.140
So this is all not for one person

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.340
but for example, this is a
IIIF image viewer right here

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:00.640
and you can hover over who's in the image

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:02.553
and understand more about the content

00:11:02.553 --> 00:11:07.553
and click on it and as well
as have all of the information

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:10.080
on how to cite the source

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.569
and give credits and attributions.

00:11:12.569 --> 00:11:15.660
And then on the very last slide,

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:18.270
it'll show you all of the
current Wikidata information

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:19.755
about them.

00:11:19.755 --> 00:11:22.813
So yeah, that's where we're headed,

00:11:22.813 --> 00:11:24.970
what we've built so far

00:11:24.970 --> 00:11:28.050
and we're planning to present this

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:30.970
the first official demo of
our first five science stories

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:32.950
at the Women's Faculty Forum in May

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:36.090
as well as we'll be
presenting the full prototype

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:39.610
at the IIIF Conference in D.C.

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:41.650
And we're submitting a
paper tonight actually

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:42.930
for presenting this

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:46.080
to the International Semantic
Web Conference in October.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:47.700
So thank you guys very much for my time.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:50.700
(audience applause)

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:56.012
- Let's just jump ahead to Amy Giuliano

00:11:56.012 --> 00:11:59.520
who's gonna be speaking on
abroad but now accessible

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:01.323
VR educational tours,

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:04.380
- My name's Amy Giuliano.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:07.610
I study religion in the visual arts

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:09.172
here at Yale University

00:12:09.172 --> 00:12:12.689
and I wanted to begin by inviting you

00:12:12.689 --> 00:12:15.750
to imagine that you're standing

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:19.113
at the threshold of St.
Peter's Basilica in Rome.

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:22.670
You see bronze doors,

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:24.140
monumental bronze doors

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:26.667
towering at five times
your height above you,

00:12:26.667 --> 00:12:30.300
and a guide beckons you into a wide nave

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.010
pierced with shafts of light

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:35.310
and immediately directs
your attention to the right

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:38.658
where you see Michelangelo's Pieta.

00:12:38.658 --> 00:12:41.900
As you walk around this masterpiece

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:44.970
and consider its various angles,

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:48.030
the guide describes to you
the genius of Michelangelo

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:49.593
and of renaissance humanism.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:53.227
So what I just described to you

00:12:53.227 --> 00:12:56.003
is the dream of one of my students,

00:12:58.683 --> 00:13:02.890
let's see, her name's Alex.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:04.680
Alex longs to study abroad in Rome

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:06.530
and especially wants to
study some of the art

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:08.460
and architecture of the city.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:10.320
However, costs and opportunity

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:12.380
prevent her from doing so.

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:14.150
And Alex is not alone.

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:18.810
In fact, less than two
percent of US college students

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:20.373
study abroad annually.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.360
The obstacles for most

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:26.119
are cost, opportunity and access.

00:13:26.119 --> 00:13:30.730
So I lived in Italy for several years,

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:31.980
I'm an Italian citizen,

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.500
I worked for years as an art
historical guide in Italy,

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:36.650
specifically in Rome

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:38.793
but some of the other major cities.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:43.670
I currently teach at
Sacred Heart University

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:44.850
in Fairfield, Connecticut

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:46.820
as an adjunct professor

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:48.435
and my frustration

00:13:48.435 --> 00:13:51.670
was that I really wanted
to share with my students

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:54.260
the transformative learning experience

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:57.170
of being within the living classroom

00:13:57.170 --> 00:14:00.135
that certain incredible cities provide.

00:14:00.135 --> 00:14:04.990
So I felt frustrated that
so many of my students

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:07.870
were missing out on this
transformative learning experience.

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:11.220
So I decided to tackle the problem head on

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:16.210
and I'll present to you
my proposed solution.

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:18.970
So two weeks ago I went to Rome

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:22.350
with some kind of high
tech photography equipment

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:25.260
and created 3D virtual reality tours

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:26.980
of some of the major sites

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:30.700
of artistic, historic
and cultural significance

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:31.603
around the city.

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:38.540
Using 3D high resolution photography,

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:42.160
laser scanning, 360 photography,

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:43.960
I virtually reconstructed

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:46.300
some of the interiors of these sites

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:49.260
and made a virtual tour.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:53.490
So I'm coupling this
immersive visual experience

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:56.000
with an interactive virtual tour.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.240
So students feel

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.840
as though they're walking
through a 3D space

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:04.135
and they can stop, click and
explore points of interest

00:15:04.135 --> 00:15:07.973
that are linked to audio,
visual and written content.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:13.300
So here are some of the
sites that I covered

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:14.643
while I was in Rome.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:18.920
Major sites of mostly
historical significance

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:22.330
but also historic and
cultural significance as well.

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:25.909
And I chose two to share with you.

00:15:25.909 --> 00:15:30.220
Here we have a classic for
any art history course,

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:32.230
the mausoleum of Santa Costanza

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:35.489
It's a fourth century building.

00:15:35.489 --> 00:15:37.350
And I'm just gonna go through,

00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:39.730
I won't give you the tour
'cause we don't have time

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:42.297
but I can explain to you
some of the advantages

00:15:42.297 --> 00:15:44.900
of using this type of technology.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:46.040
As you can see,

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:48.990
the student moves around the space

00:15:48.990 --> 00:15:53.043
and can look all the way around, oops,

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:58.273
if I can get the mouse
to work, there we go.

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:00.710
There's these buttons here

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:03.703
that allow you to zoom in
for high resolution detail.

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:07.200
You can zoom to different areas.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.623
There's a highlight reel.

00:16:11.977 --> 00:16:14.420
You can kind of see how it works here.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:19.060
So this church took about
two hours to complete.

00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:23.930
This is a replica of the
sepulchre of costanza

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:26.809
which is now in the Vatican museums.

00:16:26.809 --> 00:16:28.990
Sorry the mouse is a
little difficult on here

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:30.730
but you can kind of see what I mean.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:35.730
Here we have Santa Maria sopra Minerva

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:37.667
which is a huge church,

00:16:37.667 --> 00:16:40.480
it's a basilica in Rome.

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:44.560
It has works by Michelangelo,
Filippino Lippi,

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:49.103
the grave of Catherine of
Siena and Fra Angelico.

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:52.883
Here's a dollhouse view and a floor plan.

00:16:54.624 --> 00:16:55.457
You can see that just as an example

00:16:55.457 --> 00:16:56.570
because I'm currently working

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:59.010
on creating the interactive tours

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:03.520
with audio, visual and
textual elements added

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:05.380
that I put a little tag here.

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:08.273
And so the student can
go into that chapel,

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:14.940
look at these 14th century,

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:17.863
excuse me, 15th century frescoes, 1493.

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:21.250
I brought my students in here

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:23.070
specifically because we were reading

00:17:23.070 --> 00:17:25.610
so many Summa contra
Gentiles by Thomas Aquinas.

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:26.960
and here he is

00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:29.860
and it's an illustration of that work.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:31.280
So they'd explore these frescoes,

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:35.210
you can zoom in for very high detail

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:38.963
if the computer doesn't
crash, here we are.

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:44.349
And again, you can see
the tag that I put in

00:17:44.349 --> 00:17:46.593
is just an example,

00:17:47.761 --> 00:17:50.860
oop, there we go,

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:54.643
of you click and read
all about this space.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:18:00.040
These little areas on the floor

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:03.260
indicate places where
you can zoom in and out

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:06.793
and kind of the next step in the tour.

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:09.320
I'll bring you to the altar

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.653
and then we'll leave this space.

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:16.113
Here's Catherine of Siena's
body underneath the altar.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:27.870
And again, I'm setting this up

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:32.290
so that students can see this
in virtual reality headsets.

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:36.083
So the experience will be
really three dimensional.

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:38.813
If you turn around here,

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:45.233
this is Christ the
Redeemer by Michelangelo.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:50.080
So walking you through this,

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:53.960
I'm trying to give a
sense of the advantages

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:58.410
of using this sort of
tool, pedagogical tool.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:00.210
Let me jump back to my presentation.

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:07.380
These are my students
exploring the Minerva

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:09.543
as they read Summa contra Gentiles.

00:19:10.664 --> 00:19:11.980
And again, some of the advantages

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:15.150
include this is an immersive,
unforgettable experience,

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:17.154
it's interactive,

00:19:17.154 --> 00:19:19.380
it's engaging,

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:21.220
it does what video cannot,

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.850
you're not passively
going through a space,

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:25.190
you're really interacting with it,

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:26.672
you're exploring.

00:19:26.672 --> 00:19:28.730
It's high resolution,

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:32.160
you can explore sculptures
at different angles,

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:33.410
you can consider your scale,

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:35.710
you can consider the context of yourself

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:37.899
within this enormous space

00:19:37.899 --> 00:19:40.670
and my little plug to you

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:43.480
is please help me give access to students

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:46.793
to sites that they may
otherwise never experience.

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:51.728
And here are some of my students.

00:19:51.728 --> 00:19:53.610
I'm pleased, just to conclude,

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:56.370
I'm pleased to announce

00:19:56.370 --> 00:20:00.060
that I've received a
fellowship with Sci City

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:03.550
to work on this project for
two months over the summer

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:05.280
and kind of get it off the ground,

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:06.410
make some more tours.

00:20:06.410 --> 00:20:08.279
So thank you.

00:20:08.279 --> 00:20:11.279
(audience applause)

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:16.330
- Thanks so much to Amy.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.070
And now we'll move on to the media concept

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:22.170
which is gonna be presented
with work from Anna Shechtman

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:23.463
and Zachary Kitt.

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:27.760
- Thank you and thanks so much
to Peter and Kathy and Doug

00:20:27.760 --> 00:20:30.320
and everyone at the DH lab.

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:35.320
Unfortunately my partner Zach Kitt

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:40.010
who is at the Jackson Institute
at Yale can't be here.

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:43.445
He was a programmer before coming to Yale

00:20:43.445 --> 00:20:46.150
and he's actually just about to graduate

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:47.400
and be a programmer again

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.060
and he has been working with me,

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:53.130
I'm a PhD candidate in English literature

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:54.701
and film and media studies

00:20:54.701 --> 00:20:59.500
and I am working on a dissertation

00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:01.470
that is called the media concept

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:03.680
that's about the evolution
of the term media

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:06.209
in the second half of the 20th century,

00:21:06.209 --> 00:21:08.993
specifically about when
we enter the vernacular

00:21:08.993 --> 00:21:11.350
and the ramifications that it had

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:13.643
for different culture industries.

00:21:13.643 --> 00:21:15.550
So it's a very literal project,

00:21:15.550 --> 00:21:16.580
it's very word based.

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:19.580
And so I actually was inspired

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:21.783
to work on it and think about it

00:21:21.783 --> 00:21:23.790
because I was fooling
around on Ngram Viewer

00:21:23.790 --> 00:21:25.050
which many of you may know,

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:29.220
just google interface here.

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:32.640
You'll see that around
after world war two,

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:37.120
there's this skyrocket in the
occurrences of the word media

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:42.120
in all the texts up to
2008 that Google Books has.

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:45.930
And so I was interested in that phenomenon

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:50.443
and also interested in
some of the iterations

00:21:50.443 --> 00:21:55.392
or offshoots of the media concept

00:21:55.392 --> 00:21:59.646
and their evolutions over time.

00:21:59.646 --> 00:22:01.980
And so I wandered into the DH lab

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.660
and asked them about Ngram Viewer

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:05.640
and also if there was a way
to have more control over it

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:09.300
because it sort of just calls

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:13.930
from this massive amount of
text that is Google Books

00:22:13.930 --> 00:22:16.960
and if you actually go in

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:18.890
and it allows you to sort of click around

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:22.310
and see which books in
particular it's calling from

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:23.143
and sometimes you'll see

00:22:23.143 --> 00:22:24.670
that actually the meditative is wrong

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:25.620
and a book that it says

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:27.297
that is from 1940

00:22:29.324 --> 00:22:32.500
is in fact a book from 1812

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:34.060
that was republished in 1940,

00:22:34.060 --> 00:22:36.200
it's just not very precise.

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:37.870
And the fact that it goes up to 2008

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:39.720
is also not ideal

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:40.970
because it doesn't go up to the present

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:43.610
and that is for all sorts of reasons

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:45.920
involving copyright and law.

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:48.167
So I wanted more control

00:22:48.167 --> 00:22:52.480
over Ngram Viewer and Google Books

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:54.050
and so I wandered into the DH Lab

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:55.938
and asked them if I could get that.

00:22:55.938 --> 00:22:58.960
And they said that I would
have to build it essentially.

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:02.640
And so I learned about corpus,

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:03.473
I learned about corpora,

00:23:03.473 --> 00:23:05.285
about building your own,

00:23:05.285 --> 00:23:07.140
basically scraping text

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:09.400
from all aspects of the web

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:11.880
or if it's not digitized, digitizing it

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:16.880
and trying to create your
own database essentially

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.710
that then you can manipulate
with various different tools

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:20.940
all of which I'm still,

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:22.820
my learning curve, I will
admit, is still very steep

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:25.210
and very grateful to the patience

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:26.570
of both Zach, my assistant

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:29.380
and everyone at the DH Lab.

00:23:29.380 --> 00:23:31.053
So I'll tell you a little bit

00:23:31.053 --> 00:23:32.221
about what Zach and I have done.

00:23:32.221 --> 00:23:34.460
We started with the Vogue corpus

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:38.950
which is available through the DH Lab,

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:40.230
thankfully Yale has that.

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:45.120
And so we have all of these PDFs

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:47.700
of articles from Vogue,
from the 20th century

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:52.010
and we actually ran a Google algorithm

00:23:52.010 --> 00:23:54.020
called Word2vec.

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:57.345
Word embedding models
are as far as I can tell

00:23:57.345 --> 00:23:59.690
the most interesting aspect

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:03.670
of the textual digital humanities,

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:04.940
they're just super exciting

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:09.940
because they allow those
who have any facility

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.570
with the algorithm

00:24:12.570 --> 00:24:13.990
to track the semantic ands

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:16.360
and tactical relationships
of words over time.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:18.580
How they do that is fairly complicated

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:20.940
and I can definitely
talk to you afterwards

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:22.510
and also would absolutely point you

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:25.510
to the blog posts of
Ryan Heuser from Stanford

00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:27.010
and Ben Schmidt from Northeastern

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:30.160
who are really facile and agile

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:34.763
at writing in between the
lexicons of the humanities

00:24:37.110 --> 00:24:38.950
and of the computer sciences.

00:24:38.950 --> 00:24:43.360
But basically, they take all
of the words in your corpus

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:48.360
and they plot them visually
in a space of n dimensions,

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:52.353
multidimensional space.

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:57.110
The theory behind this is you
can actually get related words

00:24:57.110 --> 00:24:58.510
from one query.

00:24:58.510 --> 00:24:59.680
So I can query media

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:01.050
and find all the related words

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:02.360
and the principal is that

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:04.910
these semantic and syntactical evolutions

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:08.470
will be tracked by the company
that a word keeps over time.

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:12.544
And so here, we just query
media in this Vogue corpus.

00:25:12.544 --> 00:25:17.544
You'll see one of the most
interesting things for me to find

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:21.020
is that in the '40s and '50s,

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:26.020
you get a lot of let's
say not very abstract,

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:30.210
pretty concrete nouns,

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:32.200
manuscripts, criticisms, palettes,

00:25:32.200 --> 00:25:34.063
also, they're all plural.

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:37.160
And then as you move through time,

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:39.087
you start to get adjectives

00:25:39.087 --> 00:25:43.360
and also adjectives that
might qualify as singular

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:46.930
like the consolidation of
media as a singular concept,

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:49.648
cultural, historical,
racial, political, religious.

00:25:49.648 --> 00:25:50.654
To me that signals,

00:25:50.654 --> 00:25:51.668
I mean this is not,

00:25:51.668 --> 00:25:54.093
this is a small dataset
still, Vogue, right.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.490
The word media might not
show up as much in Vogue

00:25:58.490 --> 00:26:00.080
as it might in say the New York Times

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:03.403
but we're more moving in
that direction, Zach and I.

00:26:05.002 --> 00:26:07.650
So to me that signals the
consolidation of a concept

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:10.400
because you have, it's being
qualified all of a sudden

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:12.610
opposed to being substituted

00:26:12.610 --> 00:26:17.610
and metaphorically or
metonymically by other nouns.

00:26:18.902 --> 00:26:22.060
So then here's where things

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:25.443
get really kind of
psychedelic and interesting.

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:31.550
So what Word2vec allows you
to play with is arithmetic

00:26:32.102 --> 00:26:34.330
but linguistic arithmetic.

00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:36.840
So you're able, for example,

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:38.240
to go into the corpus

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:39.903
and give it an equation.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:47.403
So let's say you wanted
to see how rigorous

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:54.350
and extensive your corpus was,

00:26:54.350 --> 00:26:55.740
if it was really good.

00:26:55.740 --> 00:26:57.550
And again, this algorithm
gets more precise

00:26:57.550 --> 00:26:58.870
the more text there is,

00:26:58.870 --> 00:27:00.320
so the bigger your corpus is.

00:27:01.612 --> 00:27:03.720
You could theoretically put in this query,

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:05.610
king plus man minus woman

00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:07.830
and you would most likely get queen

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:09.640
because it's that smart

00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:10.900
which is a little menacing

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:12.656
but also really interesting.

00:27:12.656 --> 00:27:15.340
So in the Vogue corpus
just for an example,

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:19.860
if we put in this
algorithm or this equation,

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:22.250
you'll see that in 1940, 50, 60,

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:24.260
all of the first, the most related words,

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:25.770
were in fact queen.

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:27.990
Then interestingly in 1970,

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:29.380
I'm not sure why it's hairy

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:33.820
and the third most related word was queen

00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:35.860
but I also thought it was kind of awesome

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:37.300
that in the Vogue corpus

00:27:37.300 --> 00:27:41.417
the most related word that should be queen

00:27:41.417 --> 00:27:43.200
is actually Thatcher in the 1980s,

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:45.775
just a glitch of history.

00:27:45.775 --> 00:27:50.775
So then Zach and I were
just sort of playing around

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:53.630
with more queries like this

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:56.260
with, we have a big, big list of them

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:59.450
and I'm still not quite
sure what to do with them

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:01.390
but this interesting thing
that you can do essentially

00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:03.730
is if you say media minus medium

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:06.280
and you throw that into the computer,

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:11.280
you'll hopefully be able to
approximate something like,

00:28:11.284 --> 00:28:14.640
it will take away, let's say,

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:19.300
all of the words that are
both the most related to media

00:28:19.300 --> 00:28:21.130
and both the most related to medium

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:23.360
and you'll try to get to
a different type, aspect,

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:24.603
element of the concept.

00:28:25.621 --> 00:28:27.870
To me, was thinking about

00:28:28.979 --> 00:28:30.788
when I chose these
particular ones to show you,

00:28:30.788 --> 00:28:33.966
medium minus media, media
plus arts, media plus press,

00:28:33.966 --> 00:28:35.308
I was trying to sort of get

00:28:35.308 --> 00:28:36.290
at these different aspects
of the media concept,

00:28:36.290 --> 00:28:39.223
its' relation to the concept
of medium or platform,

00:28:40.858 --> 00:28:45.630
its relationship to the
phenomenon of mass culture,

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:50.630
its relationship to the article the.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:53.670
So media minus press,

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:55.143
I was sort of trying to get at

00:28:55.143 --> 00:28:58.090
if you take away the very
activity of the press

00:28:58.090 --> 00:29:00.230
is there some way to get
this sort of environmental

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:03.240
or atmospheric quality of media

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:05.458
that's not actually professional

00:29:05.458 --> 00:29:06.360
but is more sort of experiential.

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:10.923
I mean these are just
aspirational algorithms here

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:14.440
but we get some really
interesting weird stuff

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:15.840
like media minus medium,

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:19.740
the third most related word is Pachevik

00:29:19.740 --> 00:29:24.060
who was the chairman of Conde Nast

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:25.270
for much of the 20th century.

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:27.010
So that's interesting and weird.

00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:28.720
Again, this is just the Vogue corpus

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:31.295
and so where Zach and
I are going with this,

00:29:31.295 --> 00:29:32.520
this I thought was interesting

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:34.420
that media plus arts over time,

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:36.510
you start to get these sort of

00:29:36.510 --> 00:29:38.579
what we would think of as mediums.

00:29:38.579 --> 00:29:43.579
So Zach and I started working
on this with the Atlantic

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:46.690
and I'm actually now
working with Richard So

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:47.840
at the McGill

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:52.260
who has access to the High Trust databases

00:29:52.260 --> 00:29:53.760
and we're actually just sort of trying

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.720
to put together a huge magazine
corpus from the 20th century

00:29:56.720 --> 00:29:59.110
and run Word2vec on that.

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:00.840
But you'll see that the Atlantic one

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:03.780
for a variety of super
interesting reasons,

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:05.711
just didn't quite work

00:30:05.711 --> 00:30:10.014
in that it's not big enough.

00:30:10.014 --> 00:30:11.330
We're only able to,

00:30:11.330 --> 00:30:13.789
just because of time and resources,

00:30:13.789 --> 00:30:16.583
Zach's and mine, not the DH Lab's,

00:30:18.020 --> 00:30:20.890
we're not actually able
to get all of the text

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:22.437
from the Atlantic

00:30:22.437 --> 00:30:24.630
but just the text that
had the word media in them

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:25.910
and that just wasn't enough text

00:30:25.910 --> 00:30:27.870
for the Word2vec
algorithm to be effective.

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:29.260
So you get these really,

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:30.905
and the way we know that essentially,

00:30:30.905 --> 00:30:32.500
first of all, they way you know that

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:33.900
is that before 1960,

00:30:33.900 --> 00:30:36.321
there's just not even
enough text there at all

00:30:36.321 --> 00:30:38.583
to have data.

00:30:39.851 --> 00:30:43.884
Also, none of these are queen.

00:30:43.884 --> 00:30:47.360
Sometimes, country,
maintain, forage, bishop,

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:49.030
it just didn't quite work.

00:30:49.030 --> 00:30:52.360
So hopefully as we continue this project

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:54.330
and we get a bigger and bigger corpus,

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:58.000
we're hoping to actually
be able to approximate

00:30:58.000 --> 00:30:59.870
the way that the media

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:02.020
talked about media in the 20th century.

00:31:02.020 --> 00:31:06.123
So that's where we're at, yeah, thank you.

00:31:07.006 --> 00:31:10.006
(audience applause)

00:31:12.944 --> 00:31:13.777
- Thanks so much

00:31:13.777 --> 00:31:16.670
and now I think having failed once

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:19.800
at being able to get the
powerpoint working correctly,

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:22.690
I think we now can
invite up Jasper Feinberg

00:31:22.690 --> 00:31:24.270
to talk about coeducation at Yale

00:31:24.270 --> 00:31:26.064
through the eyes of the pioneer,

00:31:26.064 --> 00:31:27.614
I think I managed to get it working,

00:31:27.614 --> 00:31:28.447
thank you Jasper.

00:31:28.447 --> 00:31:29.280
- Thank you.

00:31:30.262 --> 00:31:34.170
So first of all, thank you
all for being here obviously.

00:31:34.170 --> 00:31:36.430
I'm a sophomore undergraduate at Yale

00:31:36.430 --> 00:31:37.840
studying mechanical engineering

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:39.550
and actually did this project

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:43.090
as part of a digital
humanities class at Yale

00:31:43.090 --> 00:31:45.440
which is a seminar offered
to all undergraduates.

00:31:45.440 --> 00:31:47.430
I believe some of the
teachers of that actual class

00:31:47.430 --> 00:31:48.868
are in the audience

00:31:48.868 --> 00:31:51.300
but I won't embarrass them by
pointing at them specifically.

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:52.880
So the goal of this project

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:57.470
was to show how Yale became coeducational

00:31:57.470 --> 00:31:58.970
but our true goal was to show

00:31:58.970 --> 00:32:00.970
how the use of digital multimedia

00:32:00.970 --> 00:32:03.220
could literally bring that process to life

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:04.270
and given the time frame,

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:06.530
I want to dive right into that.

00:32:06.530 --> 00:32:08.830
So our goal was to not just sort of

00:32:08.830 --> 00:32:13.600
give a textbook history of
Yale's process to coeducation

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:14.980
but to really sort of make our audience

00:32:14.980 --> 00:32:18.530
feel a lot of the actual
struggles, discrimination

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:21.280
and the context of this era.

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:22.450
And we chose to do that

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:24.500
by focusing on one person in particular

00:32:24.500 --> 00:32:27.530
as opposed to showing a broader spectrum.

00:32:27.530 --> 00:32:29.460
That person happened to be Margaret Homans

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:31.810
who is currently an English professor here

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:35.593
and was member of the second
class of women at Yale.

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:40.450
But before giving a full
rundown of her story,

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:42.256
I just want to sort of
establish the context

00:32:42.256 --> 00:32:44.390
of coeducation at Yale.

00:32:44.390 --> 00:32:45.970
So as some of you may or may not know,

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:49.700
Yale actually did not become
coeducational until 1969

00:32:49.700 --> 00:32:53.330
and it sort of became coeducational

00:32:53.330 --> 00:32:54.560
as the women's movement

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:58.500
and broader movements at Yale
itself began to push for it.

00:32:58.500 --> 00:32:59.480
But it should be noted

00:32:59.480 --> 00:33:02.180
that Yale was not exactly the pioneer

00:33:02.180 --> 00:33:03.750
despite the title of this project

00:33:03.750 --> 00:33:05.970
in the field of coeducation.

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:10.660
By 1897, 56% of all institutions

00:33:10.660 --> 00:33:12.700
were already coeducational.

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:15.240
By the time that Yale
actually became coeducational,

00:33:15.240 --> 00:33:17.560
that number was in the 80 percents.

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:19.773
So to give sort of a map,

00:33:19.773 --> 00:33:23.010
this is Yale obviously.

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:25.070
The first school to become coeducational

00:33:25.070 --> 00:33:27.423
was Oberlin in 1837.

00:33:28.300 --> 00:33:30.230
Once Oberlin became coeducational,

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:32.720
then about nine more universities

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:35.400
became coeducational in the 1850s

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:38.460
including Baylor, Lawrence, Antioch,

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:39.780
but the movement still had not started,

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:41.470
it was mostly small colleges

00:33:41.470 --> 00:33:45.680
and the true shift towards
coeducation had not begun.

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:47.840
But that became much more pronounced

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:50.180
with the beginning of the
land grant universities,

00:33:50.180 --> 00:33:51.920
especially in Western region

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:54.530
which, if you don't know,
are large public institutions

00:33:54.530 --> 00:33:56.960
that usually began coeducational

00:33:56.960 --> 00:33:58.922
and that really drove the push.

00:33:58.922 --> 00:34:03.922
HBCUs as well became
coeducational in the 1800s.

00:34:05.110 --> 00:34:07.530
Now if we fast forward a little bit,

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:12.530
by 1965, most schools
had become coeducational,

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:17.020
that included Harvard, UPenn,

00:34:17.020 --> 00:34:18.220
many of the schools in the west

00:34:18.220 --> 00:34:21.280
such as Stanford, Cal Tech, et cetera,

00:34:21.280 --> 00:34:24.261
and about overall, I think 84% of colleges

00:34:24.261 --> 00:34:26.370
were coeducational.

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:29.940
So by the time Yale actually
became coeducational in 1969

00:34:29.940 --> 00:34:32.280
they were far behind most of their peers.

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:35.660
This was not necessarily
a great achievement

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:37.200
but nonetheless,

00:34:37.200 --> 00:34:39.770
they had a very large transition to make.

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:41.500
Shifting a school from solely male

00:34:41.500 --> 00:34:43.900
to a coeducational institution

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:45.300
is no easy transition

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:47.500
and that's what we wanted to focus on

00:34:47.500 --> 00:34:49.770
through the eyes of
Margaret Homans herself

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:51.483
and looking at her experiences.

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:54.093
So with that in mind,

00:34:55.490 --> 00:34:58.170
Margaret Homans starting
with her childhood

00:34:58.170 --> 00:35:00.370
was born in Boston

00:35:00.370 --> 00:35:02.210
and she actually attended

00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:03.740
something called the Commonwealth School

00:35:03.740 --> 00:35:06.470
which is a private school in Boston

00:35:06.470 --> 00:35:08.660
and she credits that
with sort of her ability

00:35:08.660 --> 00:35:11.360
to get into a place like Yale so early,

00:35:11.360 --> 00:35:13.410
become one of the first
members of her class.

00:35:13.410 --> 00:35:15.155
Obviously, she had privileges

00:35:15.155 --> 00:35:19.360
that many other women did
not have during that era.

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:22.960
And she decided to attend Yale

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:25.470
not really because it had
just become coeducational

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:28.390
but just because of the strength
of its academic programs.

00:35:28.390 --> 00:35:30.190
She had a teacher in high school

00:35:30.190 --> 00:35:32.680
who had gone to Yale for English

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:33.890
and he sort of described to her

00:35:33.890 --> 00:35:35.810
sort of a utopia for the English language

00:35:35.810 --> 00:35:37.010
and studies of it.

00:35:37.010 --> 00:35:40.870
So she chose Yale mostly just
for its academic achievements

00:35:40.870 --> 00:35:42.550
and the fact that it
had become coeducational

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:44.646
allowed her to do that.

00:35:44.646 --> 00:35:45.770
But it was not that she
desired to be a pioneer,

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:47.763
she simply wanted to study English.

00:35:50.750 --> 00:35:53.420
So Yale, to give a
very, very brief history

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:54.640
given the time constraint,

00:35:54.640 --> 00:35:57.780
decided to admit women in the 1960s

00:35:57.780 --> 00:36:00.163
after a variety of student protests.

00:36:01.109 --> 00:36:04.080
So the main factor that
shifted during this era

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:07.550
was that Yale's actual admission
criterion began to shift.

00:36:07.550 --> 00:36:11.240
President Kingman Brewster
decided to begin to admit people

00:36:11.240 --> 00:36:14.631
based on merit as opposed
to family connections,

00:36:14.631 --> 00:36:15.960
wealth, et cetera,

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:17.910
which it seems like a
very obvious decision

00:36:17.910 --> 00:36:19.240
to us at the time

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:21.200
but earlier in this era,

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:22.880
merit had been a factor

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:25.180
but not the main factor in admissions.

00:36:25.180 --> 00:36:28.570
And so once merit became
the true determining factor

00:36:28.570 --> 00:36:30.870
between who was admitted and not admitted,

00:36:30.870 --> 00:36:33.670
then many more people
from public institutions,

00:36:33.670 --> 00:36:36.830
public high schools, Western high schools

00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:38.340
began to attend Yale

00:36:38.340 --> 00:36:40.210
and that's when it produced
the demographic shift

00:36:40.210 --> 00:36:42.090
amongst the men at Yale.

00:36:42.090 --> 00:36:44.807
And eventually that led to protests

00:36:44.807 --> 00:36:47.440
in what's called May Day

00:36:47.440 --> 00:36:49.250
which was a series of protests

00:36:49.250 --> 00:36:53.940
that tried to show Kingman Brewster

00:36:53.940 --> 00:36:55.240
that the student body

00:36:55.240 --> 00:36:58.270
was behind this shift towards coeducation.

00:36:58.270 --> 00:37:01.360
So upon feeling a lot of student pressure,

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:03.197
faculty finally decided to get behind this

00:37:03.197 --> 00:37:06.060
and they decided to create
proposals, et cetera,

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:08.540
and finally in 1969,

00:37:08.540 --> 00:37:10.620
Yale decided to become coeducational

00:37:10.620 --> 00:37:13.310
and again to establish some context,

00:37:13.310 --> 00:37:14.670
this shift was not easy.

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:16.380
We included some multimedia here

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:19.555
of the letters that were
sent to Kingman Brewster.

00:37:19.555 --> 00:37:22.410
Some calling women indignant

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:25.894
for showing their knees
and thighs in public,

00:37:25.894 --> 00:37:27.490
calling them prostitutes

00:37:27.490 --> 00:37:29.910
if they were to even
show any sort of skin,

00:37:29.910 --> 00:37:30.760
many letters saying

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:33.950
that if women dominated
Yale, Yale would end,

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:36.080
they wanted their sons
to be able to go to Yale

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:37.470
and they could not do that

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:39.807
if women somehow attended.

00:37:39.807 --> 00:37:42.260
So by the time Homans got here,

00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:45.567
it was not exactly a easy transition.

00:37:45.567 --> 00:37:47.460
And I think that's really reflected

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:49.590
in her anecdotes and her stories.

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:51.118
So starting with academics,

00:37:51.118 --> 00:37:53.180
she describes being used

00:37:53.180 --> 00:37:56.960
to being in a simply
coeducational environment.

00:37:56.960 --> 00:37:58.560
To her it was not significant

00:37:58.560 --> 00:38:00.360
that women were now in these classes.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:02.410
But to many of the boys, it was.

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:04.470
She described being in a classroom,

00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:05.600
especially in her English classes

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:07.970
and being one of only
one or two women there

00:38:07.970 --> 00:38:10.050
and how the pressure was
always on her to speak

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:13.010
whenever any topic related
to femininity came up

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:15.810
and how outside of that she
felt very reticent to speak,

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:19.023
she did not feel as though she
could really develop a voice.

00:38:19.870 --> 00:38:21.430
One of the things that
she described as lucky

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:24.400
was being in classrooms
with some female professors

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:27.900
who actually encouraged her
to study female literature

00:38:27.900 --> 00:38:29.870
and feminist critique

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:31.040
and she felt like that was really

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:32.990
the main academic experience

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:35.030
as opposed to reading just books

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:38.676
by what she described as and
I quote, "Dead white men."

00:38:38.676 --> 00:38:40.650
Outside of the classroom,

00:38:40.650 --> 00:38:42.730
Homans became involved heavily

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:45.490
in the women's abortion
referral center at Yale

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:48.037
which is currently in the,

00:38:48.037 --> 00:38:51.139
it was formerly in what
is now the AFAM House

00:38:51.139 --> 00:38:53.070
if any of you know where that is.

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:55.240
She also briefly joined the Glee Club

00:38:55.240 --> 00:38:57.200
and several other clubs on campus,

00:38:57.200 --> 00:38:58.460
although she felt like she was usually

00:38:58.460 --> 00:39:01.070
admitted to those clubs
solely because she was a woman

00:39:01.070 --> 00:39:03.124
and most of the clubs
at the time had a quota

00:39:03.124 --> 00:39:04.350
where they were supposed

00:39:04.350 --> 00:39:06.650
to try to achieve equal gender balance.

00:39:06.650 --> 00:39:08.950
So there was a lot of
pressure to admit women.

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:11.530
Some of the experiences

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:13.810
that I thought were most poignant however,

00:39:13.810 --> 00:39:15.840
were just her personal experiences.

00:39:15.840 --> 00:39:19.380
So women were all housed
in Vanderbilt Hall

00:39:19.380 --> 00:39:20.213
during this era

00:39:20.213 --> 00:39:22.620
and there was a glass
door to Vanderbilt Hall

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:25.030
and Homans described that
door as being symbolic

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:27.080
where she felt as though women could see

00:39:27.080 --> 00:39:30.560
sort of the freedom they
were promised in coeducation

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:31.920
but they couldn't actually achieve it

00:39:31.920 --> 00:39:33.750
because there was a guard at that door

00:39:33.750 --> 00:39:35.980
who wouldn't let them
out at night, et cetera,

00:39:35.980 --> 00:39:39.513
whereas none of the other halls
had a guard or such a door.

00:39:40.500 --> 00:39:41.780
Another experience she mentioned

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:44.140
was how she sewed herself

00:39:44.140 --> 00:39:45.560
what she described as a uniform

00:39:45.560 --> 00:39:47.400
which is essentially a long dress

00:39:47.400 --> 00:39:48.680
and she wore that every day

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:50.550
to essentially cover
up any part of her body

00:39:50.550 --> 00:39:52.620
because she was not used to being

00:39:52.620 --> 00:39:54.180
sort of viewed as a female body

00:39:54.180 --> 00:39:55.960
as opposed to just another student

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:57.550
and she wanted to hide herself

00:39:57.550 --> 00:39:59.793
and this uniform was a
way for her to do that.

00:39:59.793 --> 00:40:02.410
And finally, she actually described

00:40:02.410 --> 00:40:07.410
how she refused to walk
down the graduation hallway

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:10.570
in her cap and gown

00:40:10.570 --> 00:40:12.600
because she felt as though
that was encouraging

00:40:12.600 --> 00:40:13.670
an all male tradition

00:40:13.670 --> 00:40:15.330
and she wanted to rebel.

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:16.163
And on the whole,

00:40:16.163 --> 00:40:18.330
I would not describe
her transition as easy

00:40:18.330 --> 00:40:21.080
but she was able to adapt to Yale

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:23.530
and find places where she was comfortable.

00:40:23.530 --> 00:40:26.550
This heat map shows areas on campus

00:40:26.550 --> 00:40:30.957
where she felt comfortable
and uncomfortable

00:40:30.957 --> 00:40:34.850
and by sort of carving out
certain areas on campus,

00:40:34.850 --> 00:40:36.510
she was able to sort of adapt,

00:40:36.510 --> 00:40:37.850
find other women

00:40:37.850 --> 00:40:40.920
and create an environment
in which she could learn.

00:40:40.920 --> 00:40:42.300
But obviously looking forward,

00:40:42.300 --> 00:40:44.010
her experiences I think

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:46.510
are emblematic of the
path we still have to go

00:40:46.510 --> 00:40:49.690
to achieve true coeducation at Yale

00:40:49.690 --> 00:40:50.815
and I just want to leave you with the idea

00:40:50.815 --> 00:40:52.560
of what does coeducation mean?

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:54.740
Is it truly just having
women in our classrooms

00:40:54.740 --> 00:40:57.480
or is it allowing a fully immersive

00:40:57.480 --> 00:40:59.430
and equal educational experience

00:40:59.430 --> 00:41:01.140
and how can we achieve that?

00:41:01.140 --> 00:41:02.714
Thank you very much.

00:41:02.714 --> 00:41:05.714
(audience applause)

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:10.770
- Thanks very much to Jasper.

00:41:10.770 --> 00:41:13.070
And now I'd like to invite Nina Varsava up

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:14.870
who is going to speak on the relationship

00:41:14.870 --> 00:41:18.950
between the style and
impact of judicial opinions.

00:41:18.950 --> 00:41:20.670
- Okay, first of all, thank you so much

00:41:20.670 --> 00:41:22.270
to the Digital Humanities Lab

00:41:22.270 --> 00:41:24.540
for all of the help they've given me,

00:41:24.540 --> 00:41:26.630
all the support they've
given me with my research

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:28.350
and thanks especially to Doug Duhine

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:30.280
who has helped me tirelessly

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:34.540
with the minutiae of this
project and related work.

00:41:34.540 --> 00:41:36.400
I'm incredibly grateful.

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:37.370
I'm going to be talking

00:41:37.370 --> 00:41:39.870
about the relationship between the style

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:42.520
and impact of judicial opinions.

00:41:42.520 --> 00:41:45.440
So judicial opinions are
the texts that judges write

00:41:45.440 --> 00:41:48.980
in order to explain and
justify their decisions

00:41:48.980 --> 00:41:51.290
and these texts make up the common law.

00:41:51.290 --> 00:41:53.080
When judges decide new cases,

00:41:53.080 --> 00:41:56.120
they have a legal duty to
follow the rules and reasoning

00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:58.943
set out in previous opinions.

00:41:58.943 --> 00:42:02.980
The more cases that a
previous opinion appears in,

00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:06.270
the more influence it
has on the common law.

00:42:06.270 --> 00:42:09.380
So I examine whether and
how an opinion's style

00:42:09.380 --> 00:42:12.853
is related to its impact,
to its legal impact.

00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:19.690
My results show that an opinion's style

00:42:19.690 --> 00:42:21.940
is a significant predictor

00:42:23.780 --> 00:42:24.860
of its impact

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:27.330
where impact is measured
by the number of cases

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:28.293
that will cite it.

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:36.938
So to give you a brief
overview of my approach,

00:42:36.938 --> 00:42:41.900
my dataset includes almost
all majority opinions

00:42:41.900 --> 00:42:44.290
from the 10th circuit court of appeals

00:42:44.290 --> 00:42:46.270
during a 12 year period.

00:42:46.270 --> 00:42:49.020
This court is an important
federal appellate court

00:42:49.020 --> 00:42:50.860
which answers to the US supreme court,

00:42:50.860 --> 00:42:54.190
it's also the court where
Neil Gorsuch served as a judge

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:56.523
before joining the
supreme court last year.

00:42:57.390 --> 00:42:59.000
So first I derived a set

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:01.130
of measurable stylistic features

00:43:01.130 --> 00:43:03.300
from the literature on judicial writing.

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:05.630
I then used natural language processing

00:43:05.630 --> 00:43:08.280
to detect and measure these features

00:43:08.280 --> 00:43:09.430
in each of my opinions.

00:43:13.511 --> 00:43:15.600
My dependent variable

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:19.010
is the number of cases
that cite the opinion

00:43:19.010 --> 00:43:22.000
and I used this as a measure
of an opinion's impact

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:24.473
on case law or its power as a precedent.

00:43:28.240 --> 00:43:30.520
My independent variables.

00:43:30.520 --> 00:43:35.182
On the top, I've listed my
stylistic features of interest.

00:43:35.182 --> 00:43:38.453
On the bottom are my control variables.

00:43:41.010 --> 00:43:43.460
For the most part on
the stylistic features,

00:43:43.460 --> 00:43:45.960
I measure these on a per word basis

00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:49.060
meaning that for example
with citations to authority

00:43:49.060 --> 00:43:50.870
I counted how many times an opinion

00:43:50.870 --> 00:43:54.363
cites statutes and other cases per word.

00:43:56.350 --> 00:44:01.350
First, I'll give you
an overview of my data

00:44:01.410 --> 00:44:02.350
using some charts

00:44:02.350 --> 00:44:04.750
to give you a sense of what they look like

00:44:04.750 --> 00:44:07.820
and then I'll show my statistical model

00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:09.463
and my statistical results.

00:44:10.300 --> 00:44:13.040
So here you can see how judges line up

00:44:13.040 --> 00:44:15.540
on lexical diversity.

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:17.540
Lexical diversity is basically

00:44:17.540 --> 00:44:20.693
the range of vocabulary of an opinion.

00:44:21.540 --> 00:44:25.890
It's simply the number of unique
words over the total words.

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:28.180
So you can see here that the judges range

00:44:28.180 --> 00:44:33.180
from about 20% unique
words to 28% unique words.

00:44:33.820 --> 00:44:35.670
Male judges are in blue,

00:44:35.670 --> 00:44:38.183
female judges are in maroon

00:44:38.183 --> 00:44:41.033
and Gorsuch is highlighted in red.

00:44:43.070 --> 00:44:44.110
Here's informality.

00:44:44.110 --> 00:44:47.606
This is basically informal usage per word.

00:44:47.606 --> 00:44:50.130
And you can see here that the judges range

00:44:50.130 --> 00:44:53.810
from about zero on
informality to about .01

00:44:53.810 --> 00:44:56.970
which means one per 100 words is informal

00:44:56.970 --> 00:44:58.553
with Gorsuch at the high end.

00:44:59.550 --> 00:45:00.730
Here's certainty.

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:04.933
So this is terms expressing
certainty per word.

00:45:06.270 --> 00:45:07.730
So judges range on this

00:45:07.730 --> 00:45:11.863
from about one to 3.5 certainty
terms per 1,000 words.

00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:15.540
And you can see the female judges

00:45:15.540 --> 00:45:17.420
clustering on the low end of certainty

00:45:17.420 --> 00:45:18.253
but what's interesting

00:45:18.253 --> 00:45:20.950
is they also cluster on
the low end of hesitancy.

00:45:20.950 --> 00:45:25.350
So this represents hesitant
or tentative expressions

00:45:26.620 --> 00:45:29.253
and Gorsuch again is on
the high end of this.

00:45:32.820 --> 00:45:36.390
So here is my main statistical model.

00:45:36.390 --> 00:45:37.570
This is a mixed model

00:45:37.570 --> 00:45:39.460
which is a type of regression

00:45:39.460 --> 00:45:41.823
that suits the structure of my data.

00:45:43.045 --> 00:45:44.280
To give you a summary,

00:45:44.280 --> 00:45:46.380
my results show that stylistic qualities

00:45:46.380 --> 00:45:49.770
run with a case's impact
or precedential power

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:51.083
in systematic ways.

00:45:52.162 --> 00:45:56.833
My style variables are on the
top in the blue rectangle.

00:45:57.940 --> 00:46:00.150
All of these variables,

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:01.510
all of these stylistic features

00:46:01.510 --> 00:46:04.360
with the exception of passive voice

00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:08.850
are statistically significant
predictors of citations.

00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:10.730
And recall that my dependent variable

00:46:10.730 --> 00:46:13.373
is the number of cases
that cite the opinion.

00:46:15.890 --> 00:46:20.890
Most of these factors are
positively related to citations

00:46:21.120 --> 00:46:23.500
but two of them,
certainty and informality,

00:46:23.500 --> 00:46:26.423
are negative predictors of citations.

00:46:28.250 --> 00:46:30.010
So I'll take my remaining time

00:46:30.010 --> 00:46:31.530
to talk through these results

00:46:31.530 --> 00:46:33.760
since it's not altogether obvious I think

00:46:33.760 --> 00:46:36.080
just from looking at the
values what they mean

00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:39.930
because for one, my dependent
variable is log transformed.

00:46:39.930 --> 00:46:42.300
So as word count goes up by one,

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:44.380
citing cases goes up by,

00:46:44.380 --> 00:46:47.160
sorry, as word count
goes up by one percent,

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:50.010
citing cases also goes
up by about one percent.

00:46:50.010 --> 00:46:53.207
As reading level goes
up by one grade level,

00:46:53.207 --> 00:46:55.793
citations goes up by five percent.

00:46:57.040 --> 00:46:59.540
As lexical diversity
goes up by one percent,

00:46:59.540 --> 00:47:01.800
cites go up by two percent.

00:47:01.800 --> 00:47:04.840
As authority per word goes up by .01,

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:07.890
so this means one additional citation

00:47:07.890 --> 00:47:11.470
to a case or statute per 100 words,

00:47:11.470 --> 00:47:14.730
cites goes up by 27%.

00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:16.930
As references to people by proper name

00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:18.690
goes up by one in 100,

00:47:18.690 --> 00:47:21.060
citing cases goes up by 20%

00:47:21.060 --> 00:47:23.630
and that's the same for
references to litigants

00:47:23.630 --> 00:47:26.320
by technical terms,
plaintiff or defendant.

00:47:26.320 --> 00:47:28.460
So it seems that just referring to people

00:47:28.460 --> 00:47:29.730
regardless of how,

00:47:29.730 --> 00:47:31.780
is a good thing for citations.

00:47:31.780 --> 00:47:36.700
As certainty per word goes
up by .001, one in 1,000,

00:47:36.700 --> 00:47:39.440
citing cases goes down by four percent

00:47:39.440 --> 00:47:42.264
but as hesitancy goes
up by the same amount,

00:47:42.264 --> 00:47:44.260
one in 1,000,

00:47:44.260 --> 00:47:45.430
citing cases goes up.

00:47:45.430 --> 00:47:48.590
So certainty is a negative
predictor of citations

00:47:48.590 --> 00:47:50.633
but hesitancy is a positive predictor.

00:47:52.210 --> 00:47:55.069
Semicolons goes up by one in 1,000,

00:47:55.069 --> 00:47:56.390
cite goes up by three percent.

00:47:56.390 --> 00:48:01.390
And then as informality per
word goes up by one in 100,

00:48:02.720 --> 00:48:05.560
citing cases goes down by 20%.

00:48:05.560 --> 00:48:08.980
So informality is strongly
negatively related

00:48:08.980 --> 00:48:10.220
with citations.

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:11.730
So to just wrap up,

00:48:11.730 --> 00:48:13.420
I also ran a regression,

00:48:13.420 --> 00:48:16.510
a normal regression with
judged fixed effects

00:48:16.510 --> 00:48:19.290
which generated very similar
results to this model.

00:48:19.290 --> 00:48:20.968
The fixed effects model

00:48:20.968 --> 00:48:24.430
shows that my variables explain 45%

00:48:24.430 --> 00:48:26.350
of the variance in citations

00:48:26.350 --> 00:48:28.690
and that my stylistic variables alone,

00:48:28.690 --> 00:48:30.710
just these 11 stylistic features

00:48:30.710 --> 00:48:33.760
explain 15% of the variance

00:48:33.760 --> 00:48:36.380
in the number of cases
that will cite an opinion.

00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:37.519
Thank you.

00:48:37.519 --> 00:48:40.519
(audience applause)

00:48:44.130 --> 00:48:45.470
- Thanks very much to Nina.

00:48:45.470 --> 00:48:47.320
And now I'd like to invite up

00:48:47.320 --> 00:48:49.810
participants in our project
called Digital Humanity,

00:48:49.810 --> 00:48:54.810
Jessica Ambrosio and Maria
Gargiulo, apologies for that.

00:48:57.157 --> 00:48:58.600
- Good morning everyone.

00:48:58.600 --> 00:48:59.970
My name is Jessica Ambrosio

00:48:59.970 --> 00:49:02.400
and I'm a junior major
in computer science.

00:49:02.400 --> 00:49:04.410
- Hi, my name's Maria Gargiulo

00:49:04.410 --> 00:49:05.650
and I'm also a junior

00:49:05.650 --> 00:49:08.280
majoring in statistics and
data science and Spanish

00:49:08.280 --> 00:49:11.260
and we're the co presidents
of Yale Code For Good.

00:49:11.260 --> 00:49:14.490
Today we're presenting on
what we call digital humanity

00:49:14.490 --> 00:49:17.037
and we'd like to begin
with this brief anecdote.

00:49:17.037 --> 00:49:18.807
"The first program most people write

00:49:18.807 --> 00:49:20.717
"is a simple, hollow world.

00:49:20.717 --> 00:49:22.647
"It's time we start seriously considering

00:49:22.647 --> 00:49:23.927
"and talking about

00:49:23.927 --> 00:49:25.527
"the impact that our code can

00:49:25.527 --> 00:49:28.127
"and does have on the
world in which we live."

00:49:29.136 --> 00:49:32.190
- So as a club we have come
to define Coding for Good

00:49:32.190 --> 00:49:34.300
as applying tools and techniques

00:49:34.300 --> 00:49:37.185
to solve problems that a
community or society faces.

00:49:37.185 --> 00:49:38.730
So what this means

00:49:38.730 --> 00:49:41.848
is that we seek to design
and develop programs

00:49:41.848 --> 00:49:44.640
that not only address the
technical aspects of programs

00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:45.590
but also consider

00:49:45.590 --> 00:49:48.010
the impact that these
technologies will have

00:49:48.010 --> 00:49:49.470
on the people who make use of them.

00:49:49.470 --> 00:49:50.890
Is the impact positive?

00:49:50.890 --> 00:49:52.460
Does it benefit them beyond

00:49:52.460 --> 00:49:54.610
just merely solely
being a technical issue?

00:49:56.410 --> 00:49:58.215
- So you might be asking now,

00:49:58.215 --> 00:50:00.288
how do you do that?

00:50:00.288 --> 00:50:03.870
So we do this by recruiting
teams of four to six students

00:50:03.870 --> 00:50:07.670
to work in collaboration with
non profit organizations,

00:50:07.670 --> 00:50:11.010
Yale student groups or
local government agencies

00:50:11.010 --> 00:50:12.740
to address socially conscious problems.

00:50:12.740 --> 00:50:15.060
And these can cover all
types of different things.

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:18.490
There's no one problem that is
or is not socially conscious,

00:50:18.490 --> 00:50:20.570
many things are different in many ways.

00:50:20.570 --> 00:50:22.480
We currently have three project teams,

00:50:22.480 --> 00:50:24.830
two of who's work we'd
like to highlight today.

00:50:25.750 --> 00:50:29.370
- So our first project
is the Roadside Manager

00:50:29.370 --> 00:50:32.285
and for this project we
partnered with (mumbles)

00:50:32.285 --> 00:50:33.840
in downtown New Haven

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:36.000
and the New Haven
Department of Transportation

00:50:36.000 --> 00:50:38.480
to address transportation traffic issues.

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:39.580
The main problem to solve here

00:50:39.580 --> 00:50:41.863
was that commuters did not know

00:50:41.863 --> 00:50:43.430
where how many parking
spaces were available

00:50:43.430 --> 00:50:44.923
at a given location.

00:50:44.923 --> 00:50:46.489
To solve this problem,

00:50:46.489 --> 00:50:49.010
(mumbles) modified the
hardware of multiple road signs

00:50:49.010 --> 00:50:50.720
so that they could be programmable.

00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:53.670
Nevertheless, someone still
had to go down to the roadside

00:50:53.670 --> 00:50:54.913
and change it manually.

00:50:55.840 --> 00:50:58.533
To this end, our team
built a web application

00:50:58.533 --> 00:50:59.983
that connects the road signs

00:50:59.983 --> 00:51:02.100
so that officials can simply
access the web application

00:51:02.100 --> 00:51:05.210
and update any information on
the road sign in real time.

00:51:05.210 --> 00:51:07.800
In addition to improving the
parking system in New Haven,

00:51:07.800 --> 00:51:10.370
this offer also provides a great mechanism

00:51:10.370 --> 00:51:13.320
for important updates
in case of emergencies.

00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:15.070
If you want to hear
more about this project,

00:51:15.070 --> 00:51:15.990
the team who worked on it

00:51:15.990 --> 00:51:19.000
will be later presenting
during the poster session.

00:51:21.750 --> 00:51:23.960
- The next project that we'd
like to talk about today

00:51:23.960 --> 00:51:26.980
has been done in collaboration
with Khushi Baby.

00:51:26.980 --> 00:51:29.460
So Khushi Baby is actually
a nonprofit organization

00:51:29.460 --> 00:51:30.620
founded at Yale,

00:51:30.620 --> 00:51:33.250
started in the CID as
part of a class project

00:51:33.250 --> 00:51:36.100
for I think a mechanical
engineering course a few years ago.

00:51:36.100 --> 00:51:37.895
It operates in India.

00:51:37.895 --> 00:51:39.500
In founding our organization,

00:51:39.500 --> 00:51:40.830
the Khushi Baby team sought

00:51:40.830 --> 00:51:44.000
to address issues in the
Indian medical system.

00:51:44.000 --> 00:51:47.250
Currently, doctors are
required to keep paper records.

00:51:47.250 --> 00:51:48.470
An issue with these records

00:51:48.470 --> 00:51:51.360
is that they need to travel
with the doctors as they move.

00:51:51.360 --> 00:51:53.010
They often become disorganized

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:54.960
and files get lost

00:51:54.960 --> 00:51:56.980
and this is particularly problematic

00:51:56.980 --> 00:51:59.450
for women and their newborn children.

00:51:59.450 --> 00:52:00.283
The issue with this

00:52:00.283 --> 00:52:02.160
is that they need to see doctors

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:04.110
at certain points post birth

00:52:04.110 --> 00:52:06.343
and if doctors are losing files,

00:52:06.343 --> 00:52:09.440
there's no record of which
vaccinations they've received

00:52:09.440 --> 00:52:11.690
when they've gone to past appointments.

00:52:11.690 --> 00:52:13.490
So to deal with this, the Khushi Baby team

00:52:13.490 --> 00:52:14.500
made a simple wearable,

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:16.350
it's a little necklace
that the child wears

00:52:16.350 --> 00:52:18.610
and it stores all of
their information in it.

00:52:18.610 --> 00:52:21.050
When doctors go to see the
children and their mothers

00:52:21.050 --> 00:52:24.100
they can simply load their
data into a web application

00:52:24.100 --> 00:52:25.300
if they have internet access

00:52:25.300 --> 00:52:27.660
and if not, there's an Android app.

00:52:27.660 --> 00:52:29.711
Multiple people can view the information

00:52:29.711 --> 00:52:34.190
when they're seeing the child
offline whenever they need to

00:52:34.190 --> 00:52:35.580
and this is really helpful

00:52:35.580 --> 00:52:39.070
because it deals with
the missing record issue

00:52:39.070 --> 00:52:43.980
and also lets people look at
information in various places.

00:52:43.980 --> 00:52:46.510
The Code for Good team has been
contributing to this project

00:52:46.510 --> 00:52:49.410
by helping Khushi Baby
update some of their models.

00:52:49.410 --> 00:52:51.270
One of the big things
that the team cares about

00:52:51.270 --> 00:52:53.190
is risk assessment.

00:52:53.190 --> 00:52:54.830
They like to triage their patients

00:52:54.830 --> 00:52:56.910
so that newborns and their mothers

00:52:56.910 --> 00:52:58.460
who are particularly high risk

00:52:58.460 --> 00:53:00.130
for various types of health issues

00:53:00.130 --> 00:53:03.333
can be viewed according
to the relevant priority.

00:53:04.260 --> 00:53:05.880
So yeah, and this is just a little image

00:53:05.880 --> 00:53:06.810
of what the dashboard

00:53:06.810 --> 00:53:09.010
of the Khushi Baby application looks like.

00:53:10.113 --> 00:53:12.127
Now you might be asking yourself,

00:53:12.127 --> 00:53:15.371
why do we do this, why
was Code for Good founded?

00:53:15.371 --> 00:53:17.810
So Code for Good was founded last spring

00:53:17.810 --> 00:53:19.670
and the reason it was founded

00:53:19.670 --> 00:53:22.490
is because there was no real
space available on campus

00:53:22.490 --> 00:53:24.380
for technically inclined students

00:53:24.380 --> 00:53:27.120
to work on socially conscious problems.

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:29.360
- So there are a lot of wonderful places

00:53:29.360 --> 00:53:31.610
where our students can go and work at

00:53:31.610 --> 00:53:33.670
but the impact that they can make there

00:53:33.670 --> 00:53:36.653
will rarely reach the people
who need their help the most.

00:53:38.090 --> 00:53:40.750
So this is your typical job fair,

00:53:40.750 --> 00:53:42.980
these are the kind of
companies that we usually see

00:53:42.980 --> 00:53:46.100
that any CS or data
science major will look at

00:53:46.100 --> 00:53:49.483
and you know what, these are
wonderful places to work at,

00:53:49.483 --> 00:53:52.400
they are doing amazing stuff

00:53:52.400 --> 00:53:55.440
but they only present a partial image

00:53:55.440 --> 00:53:58.393
of what can be done with technology.

00:54:00.550 --> 00:54:01.930
- And to end off today,

00:54:01.930 --> 00:54:03.897
we'd like to leave you all with a question

00:54:03.897 --> 00:54:05.950
or perhaps a challenge.

00:54:05.950 --> 00:54:07.070
How can you do more good

00:54:07.070 --> 00:54:09.670
with the technology that
you use or know how to use?

00:54:11.320 --> 00:54:13.320
- So if you have an
answer to this question

00:54:13.320 --> 00:54:15.030
or just want to share your ideas with us

00:54:15.030 --> 00:54:17.960
please contact us at codeforgood@yale.edu.

00:54:17.960 --> 00:54:19.701
- [Both] Thank you.

00:54:19.701 --> 00:54:22.701
(audience applause)

00:54:28.170 --> 00:54:29.640
- Thank you, Jessica and Maria

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:31.260
and to all of our presenters

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:33.600
during this first initial lightning talks

00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:34.870
part of the conference.

00:54:34.870 --> 00:54:35.730
I think at this point,

00:54:35.730 --> 00:54:39.269
I'd like to invite up members
of our round table discussion.

00:54:39.269 --> 00:54:41.936
(playful music)

